1
00:02:57,470 --> 00:02:59,179
(THUNDER RUMBLING)

2
00:03:53,651 --> 00:03:54,984
(SCREAMING)

3
00:03:58,614 --> 00:04:00,448
(ALARM CLOCK BEEPING)

4
00:04:10,918 --> 00:04:12,252
Claire.

5
00:04:27,393 --> 00:04:28,852
I miss you.

6
00:05:36,670 --> 00:05:40,131
<i>WOMAN OVER PA: Dr Weir, report to
the Lewis & Clark in Docking Bay 4.</i>

7
00:05:41,050 --> 00:05:42,634
(ENGINES BLASTING)

8
00:05:58,025 --> 00:06:00,401
<i>JUSTIN: lon drive set.
SMITH: Thank you.</i>

9
00:06:02,446 --> 00:06:06,074
Heading: Vector One, nine degrees.

10
00:06:10,913 --> 00:06:14,082
We are prepped for lockdown.

11
00:06:17,795 --> 00:06:20,296
I can't believe it, this is ridiculous.

12
00:06:20,381 --> 00:06:25,301
I haven't got more than my hand
in the last six weeks, and now this shit.

13
00:06:25,386 --> 00:06:27,512
I mean,
why can't we go to Mars, Captain?

14
00:06:27,596 --> 00:06:32,016
- I mean, Mars has got women.
- Smith's right.

15
00:06:32,101 --> 00:06:33,768
Neptune, there's nothing out there.

16
00:06:33,852 --> 00:06:36,104
Yeah, and if the shit goes down,
we'll be on our own.

17
00:06:36,188 --> 00:06:37,897
You know the rules, people.

18
00:06:37,982 --> 00:06:42,026
Someone drops the ball, we get the call.
Now, let's go.

19
00:06:42,528 --> 00:06:44,237
- Have you got our course plotted in?
- Yes, sir.

20
00:06:44,321 --> 00:06:47,365
- Locked and cocked and ready to rock.
- Mr Justin?

21
00:06:48,492 --> 00:06:50,076
Everything's green on my screen,
Skipper.

22
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:51,327
Start your countdown now.

23
00:06:51,412 --> 00:06:54,497
Lon drive will engage
in T minus 10 minutes.

24
00:06:54,582 --> 00:06:56,291
Thank you, Lieutenant.

25
00:06:56,375 --> 00:06:57,542
Let's go.

26
00:06:57,626 --> 00:07:00,461
- Smith, you follow me.
- Yes, ma'am.

27
00:07:00,546 --> 00:07:03,590
- Justin, get those tanks prepped.
- Yes, sir.

28
00:07:07,511 --> 00:07:09,220
- Mr Cooper.
- Skipper.

29
00:07:09,305 --> 00:07:12,640
- Lon drive in 10 minutes.
- It's time to play spam-in-the-can.

30
00:07:12,725 --> 00:07:15,310
- Skipper.
- Peters.

31
00:07:16,061 --> 00:07:18,187
Captain Miller,
I just wanted to say this is something...

32
00:07:18,272 --> 00:07:20,398
Clock is ticking, Doctor.

33
00:07:22,192 --> 00:07:24,485
You waiting for a personal invitation,
there, Mr Smith?

34
00:07:24,570 --> 00:07:26,237
- No, sir.
- Captain, I don't want to take up...

35
00:07:26,322 --> 00:07:29,115
Doctor, if you'll just follow
the rest of the crew to the grav tanks,

36
00:07:29,199 --> 00:07:30,450
we're almost underway.

37
00:07:30,534 --> 00:07:32,452
That bunk better be squared away,
Mr Cooper,

38
00:07:32,536 --> 00:07:34,245
or you'll find yourself walking
to Neptune.

39
00:07:34,330 --> 00:07:35,622
What's the hold-up, Peters?

40
00:07:35,706 --> 00:07:38,625
Just loading
the last of the CO2 scrubbers, sir.

41
00:07:38,792 --> 00:07:40,668
You stand right here.

42
00:07:43,505 --> 00:07:44,756
Thanks, D.J.

43
00:07:45,090 --> 00:07:46,507
- Hats off in the tank.
- Sir.

44
00:07:46,592 --> 00:07:49,260
- First time in a grav couch?
- Yeah.

45
00:07:49,345 --> 00:07:50,553
Uh-huh.

46
00:07:51,138 --> 00:07:54,474
I think your captain
has some kind of a problem with me.

47
00:07:54,558 --> 00:07:56,768
Oh, well. Don't worry about him.

48
00:07:56,852 --> 00:07:59,270
He just loves having complete strangers
on his ship.

49
00:07:59,355 --> 00:08:01,230
- D.J.
- Yeah.

50
00:08:03,734 --> 00:08:04,817
No.

51
00:08:04,902 --> 00:08:06,819
Is this necessary?

52
00:08:06,904 --> 00:08:10,823
When the ion drive fires,
you'll be taking about 30 G's.

53
00:08:11,575 --> 00:08:15,161
Without a tank,
the force would liquefy your skeleton.

54
00:08:16,497 --> 00:08:19,082
I've seen the effect on mice.

55
00:08:22,878 --> 00:08:24,295
Claustrophobic?

56
00:08:24,838 --> 00:08:26,047
Very.

57
00:08:50,739 --> 00:08:52,156
<i>CLAIRE: Billy.</i>

58
00:09:34,992 --> 00:09:37,952
<i>CLAIRE: I'm so alone.</i>

59
00:09:40,873 --> 00:09:42,123
Hello?

60
00:09:51,258 --> 00:09:52,967
Is anyone there?

61
00:10:02,853 --> 00:10:03,853
Oh, God.

62
00:10:36,887 --> 00:10:39,013
<i>Billy?</i>

63
00:10:40,557 --> 00:10:42,225
<i>I'm so cold.</i>

64
00:10:52,027 --> 00:10:53,444
Claire.

65
00:10:56,073 --> 00:10:57,406
I'm waiting.

66
00:10:57,491 --> 00:10:58,908
(SCREAMING)

67
00:11:10,087 --> 00:11:13,297
You're all right. Breathe. D.J.!

68
00:11:14,424 --> 00:11:16,342
- Okay, Dr Weir, let's get you up.
- Okay, I'm fine.

69
00:11:16,426 --> 00:11:17,677
- Come on, take it easy.
- I'm fine.

70
00:11:17,761 --> 00:11:20,429
Okay. There you go.

71
00:11:21,265 --> 00:11:24,684
Now move nice and slow.
You've been in stasis for 56 days.

72
00:11:24,768 --> 00:11:27,478
You're gonna experience
a little disorientation, you understand?

73
00:11:27,563 --> 00:11:31,315
- I'm all right now, thank you.
- Okay, take that. Don't rush it.

74
00:11:31,608 --> 00:11:33,276
All yours.

75
00:11:37,114 --> 00:11:38,781
You okay, Doc?

76
00:11:39,783 --> 00:11:42,451
- Want some coffee?
- What?

77
00:11:43,328 --> 00:11:45,997
Would you like some coffee?

78
00:11:47,165 --> 00:11:49,500
No. Thank you.

79
00:11:50,002 --> 00:11:52,503
All right, suit yourself.

80
00:11:52,629 --> 00:11:54,046
What about you, Starck?

81
00:11:54,131 --> 00:11:56,757
Would you like
something hot and black inside you?

82
00:11:56,842 --> 00:11:59,552
- (EXCLAIMS) Is that an offer?
- It is not.

83
00:11:59,636 --> 00:12:02,763
- Then how about some coffee?
- Starck?

84
00:12:02,848 --> 00:12:05,057
- Yes, sir.
- Why aren't you on the bridge?

85
00:12:05,142 --> 00:12:06,392
Do you mind if I get dressed first?

86
00:12:06,476 --> 00:12:09,228
As a matter of fact, Lieutenant,
I do mind if you get dressed first.

87
00:12:09,313 --> 00:12:11,731
Let's go, people. We have a job to do.

88
00:12:12,190 --> 00:12:15,735
<i>PETERS: Okay, Denny, here we go.
Happy birthday.</i>

89
00:12:15,819 --> 00:12:18,029
<i>Play horsey, Mommy. Play horsey.</i>

90
00:12:18,155 --> 00:12:20,489
Hey, no more ball in the house.

91
00:12:21,450 --> 00:12:23,492
You're the one who can't catch.

92
00:12:23,619 --> 00:12:27,496
- Yo, apologise.
- Sorry about that, Mama Bear.

93
00:12:27,623 --> 00:12:29,081
- All right.
- All right, then.

94
00:12:29,166 --> 00:12:31,042
- Peters.
- Yes, sir.

95
00:12:31,627 --> 00:12:33,920
I did try to find a replacement for you,

96
00:12:34,004 --> 00:12:36,756
but with such short notice,
it was impossible.

97
00:12:36,840 --> 00:12:38,215
I am sorry.

98
00:12:38,342 --> 00:12:42,261
I talked to my ex.
He's going to take Denny for Christmas.

99
00:12:42,346 --> 00:12:45,473
I'll get him for the summer, so

100
00:12:45,557 --> 00:12:47,224
everything's all right.

101
00:12:47,476 --> 00:12:48,851
Smitty.

102
00:12:49,186 --> 00:12:51,062
Excuse me, ladies and gentlemen,

103
00:12:51,146 --> 00:12:52,939
in approximately
2 hours and 23 minutes,

104
00:12:53,023 --> 00:12:56,067
we'll be arriving in the orbit of Neptune.
Thank you very much.

105
00:12:56,151 --> 00:12:58,361
- I thank you.
- That's good.

106
00:12:58,487 --> 00:13:01,280
All boards are green.
Everything's five by five.

107
00:13:01,365 --> 00:13:03,908
- U.S.A.C. been notified of our position?
- Yes, sir.

108
00:13:03,992 --> 00:13:05,743
- All right, people, listen up.
- D.J.

109
00:13:05,827 --> 00:13:07,912
As you all know,
we have an addition to our crew.

110
00:13:07,996 --> 00:13:10,331
Dr Weir, you care to join us?

111
00:13:10,999 --> 00:13:14,502
Introductions all around.
This is my XO, Lieutenant Starck.

112
00:13:14,586 --> 00:13:16,253
Mr Justin, Engineering.

113
00:13:16,380 --> 00:13:18,005
The funky spaceman over here
is Mr Cooper.

114
00:13:18,090 --> 00:13:20,299
What exactly is it you do
on board this ship, Coop?

115
00:13:20,384 --> 00:13:23,052
Listen up, Doc,
I'm your best friend, okay?

116
00:13:23,178 --> 00:13:25,513
I am the lifesaver and the heartbreaker.

117
00:13:25,597 --> 00:13:27,431
He's a rescue technician.

118
00:13:27,557 --> 00:13:31,602
This is Peters, medical technician.
And my pilot, Mr Smith.

119
00:13:33,063 --> 00:13:35,231
The gloomy Gus
in the corner over there, that's...

120
00:13:35,315 --> 00:13:37,650
D.J. Trauma.

121
00:13:37,776 --> 00:13:39,110
(LAUGHING)

122
00:13:39,903 --> 00:13:41,404
All right, all right.

123
00:13:41,488 --> 00:13:44,615
Now we all know each other.
Skipper, I got a question.

124
00:13:44,741 --> 00:13:46,659
What the fuck
are we doing way out here?

125
00:13:46,743 --> 00:13:50,246
Perhaps the good doctor
will be kind enough to tell us.

126
00:13:50,580 --> 00:13:51,956
Thank you.

127
00:13:52,749 --> 00:13:54,834
First of all,
I want to say how much I appreciate

128
00:13:54,918 --> 00:13:57,211
- this opportunity to join you on what I...
- It's for sure

129
00:13:57,295 --> 00:14:00,673
that you appreciate being here, Doctor,
but you must understand that we don't.

130
00:14:00,757 --> 00:14:02,550
We were taken off
a well-deserved leave

131
00:14:02,634 --> 00:14:04,176
and sent into Neptune space.

132
00:14:04,261 --> 00:14:07,763
We are now three billion klicks
from the nearest outpost.

133
00:14:08,181 --> 00:14:11,684
The last time U.S.A.C. attempted
a rescue this far past the outer reach,

134
00:14:11,768 --> 00:14:15,021
well, we lost both ships.
So, if you please...

135
00:14:15,355 --> 00:14:17,023
Right. Well...

136
00:14:18,025 --> 00:14:21,819
Everything I'm about to tell you
is considered Code Black by the NSA.

137
00:14:21,945 --> 00:14:24,113
U.S.A.C. intercepted
a radio transmission

138
00:14:24,197 --> 00:14:26,949
from a decaying orbit around Neptune.

139
00:14:27,993 --> 00:14:31,620
The source of this transmission
has been identified

140
00:14:31,705 --> 00:14:33,622
<i>as the Event Horizon.</i>

141
00:14:35,959 --> 00:14:38,044
Yeah, well, that's bullshit, for starters.

142
00:14:38,128 --> 00:14:41,297
- Skipper, you took me off leave...
- Thank you so much.

143
00:14:41,381 --> 00:14:43,174
Smitty, sit down.

144
00:14:44,676 --> 00:14:47,011
Cooper, as you were.

145
00:14:48,138 --> 00:14:49,388
Let the man speak.

146
00:14:49,473 --> 00:14:51,390
What was made public
about the Event Horizon,

147
00:14:51,475 --> 00:14:53,893
that she was a deep space
research vessel,

148
00:14:53,977 --> 00:14:58,064
that her reactor went critical,
and that the ship blew up,

149
00:14:58,148 --> 00:14:59,732
none of that is true.

150
00:14:59,816 --> 00:15:03,235
The Event Horizon was the culmination
of a secret government project

151
00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:06,822
to create a spacecraft
capable of faster-than-light flight.

152
00:15:06,907 --> 00:15:09,992
I don't... Excuse me.
See, you can't actually do that.

153
00:15:10,077 --> 00:15:13,829
The law of relativity
prohibits faster-than-light travel.

154
00:15:14,498 --> 00:15:18,084
Relativity, yes.
We can't break the law of relativity.

155
00:15:19,002 --> 00:15:20,503
We can go around it.

156
00:15:20,879 --> 00:15:22,588
The ship doesn't really go faster
than light.

157
00:15:22,672 --> 00:15:25,674
What it does is
it creates a dimensional gateway

158
00:15:25,759 --> 00:15:27,843
that allows it to jump instantaneously

159
00:15:27,928 --> 00:15:30,346
from one point of the universe
to another light years away.

160
00:15:30,430 --> 00:15:31,680
How?

161
00:15:32,099 --> 00:15:35,893
- Well, it's difficult to... It's all math.
- Try us, Doctor.

162
00:15:37,354 --> 00:15:39,897
Right, well, using layman's terms,

163
00:15:40,398 --> 00:15:42,608
we use a rotating magnetic field

164
00:15:42,692 --> 00:15:46,946
to focus a narrow beam of gravitons.
These in turn fold space-time,

165
00:15:47,030 --> 00:15:49,115
consistent with Weyl tensor dynamics,

166
00:15:49,199 --> 00:15:51,951
until the space-time curvature
becomes infinitely large

167
00:15:52,035 --> 00:15:54,537
and you produce a singularity.
Now, the singularity...

168
00:15:54,621 --> 00:15:56,372
Layman's terms.

169
00:15:56,456 --> 00:15:59,416
Fuck layman's terms.
Do you speak English?

170
00:16:03,588 --> 00:16:06,549
Imagine, for a minute,
that this piece of paper...

171
00:16:06,633 --> 00:16:10,052
Excuse me.
That's Vanessa, and that's mine.

172
00:16:10,137 --> 00:16:12,930
Attractive piece of paper
represents space-time

173
00:16:13,056 --> 00:16:17,268
and you want to get from
point "A" here

174
00:16:17,394 --> 00:16:19,186
to "B" there.

175
00:16:19,271 --> 00:16:22,356
Now, what's the shortest distance
between two points?

176
00:16:22,440 --> 00:16:23,899
A straight line.

177
00:16:24,651 --> 00:16:25,734
Wrong.

178
00:16:25,819 --> 00:16:28,779
The shortest distance
between two points is zero.

179
00:16:28,905 --> 00:16:30,990
And that's what the gateway does.

180
00:16:31,241 --> 00:16:34,493
It folds space.

181
00:16:34,578 --> 00:16:39,331
So that point "A" and point "B" co-exist
in the same space and time.

182
00:16:40,292 --> 00:16:44,378
When the spacecraft passes
through the gateway,

183
00:16:44,462 --> 00:16:46,505
space returns to normal.

184
00:16:46,923 --> 00:16:49,258
It's called a gravity drive.

185
00:16:50,302 --> 00:16:53,804
- How do you know all this?
- I built it.

186
00:16:54,472 --> 00:16:56,432
Well, I can see why they sent you.

187
00:16:56,516 --> 00:16:59,226
So if the ship didn't blow up,
then what happened?

188
00:16:59,311 --> 00:17:03,355
Well, the mission was going perfectly,
like a textbook.

189
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:05,941
They reached safe distance
using conventional thrusters.

190
00:17:06,026 --> 00:17:07,902
All systems looked good.

191
00:17:07,986 --> 00:17:09,945
They had the go-ahead to use
the gravity drive

192
00:17:10,030 --> 00:17:12,698
to open the gateway
to Proxima Centauri

193
00:17:13,283 --> 00:17:16,160
and then, they just disappeared.

194
00:17:16,369 --> 00:17:20,080
Vanished without a trace, until now.

195
00:17:20,165 --> 00:17:23,167
Where's she been
for the last seven years, Doctor?

196
00:17:23,710 --> 00:17:26,295
That's what we're here to find out.

197
00:17:26,379 --> 00:17:29,632
Now, we've been unable
to verify life contact,

198
00:17:30,634 --> 00:17:34,345
but TDRS did receive
this single transmission.

199
00:17:36,264 --> 00:17:37,640
<i>(SCREAMING)</i>

200
00:17:38,850 --> 00:17:40,559
<i>(CREATURE ROARING)</i>

201
00:17:45,023 --> 00:17:47,566
- What the fucking hell is that?
- Listen to this.

202
00:17:47,651 --> 00:17:51,153
Houston passed the same recording
through several filters,

203
00:17:51,238 --> 00:17:55,366
managed to isolate what appears to be
a human voice.

204
00:17:55,951 --> 00:17:58,327
<i>(INCOMPREHENSIBLE
STATICKY VOICE)</i>

205
00:18:03,375 --> 00:18:05,793
I'm not even sure
if it qualifies as language.

206
00:18:05,877 --> 00:18:07,920
- Latin.
- What?

207
00:18:08,505 --> 00:18:11,173
I mean, I think it sounds like Latin.

208
00:18:11,258 --> 00:18:14,009
- Can you translate it?
- Play it again.

209
00:18:18,139 --> 00:18:20,683
Right there. You hear that?

210
00:18:23,561 --> 00:18:27,940
Sounds like, "Liberate me,"
something or other.

211
00:18:28,024 --> 00:18:29,066
I can't make out the rest.

212
00:18:29,150 --> 00:18:30,901
<i>"Liberate me?"</i>

213
00:18:31,987 --> 00:18:33,696
Save me.

214
00:18:33,780 --> 00:18:35,406
<i>(BUZZER SOUNDING)</i>

215
00:18:35,657 --> 00:18:37,908
All right. Stations, people.

216
00:18:51,506 --> 00:18:55,718
Crossing the Horizon.
Optimum approach angle is 14 degrees.

217
00:18:55,802 --> 00:18:59,305
- Come around to 334.
- Heading 334, sir.

218
00:18:59,389 --> 00:19:02,141
Make your approach vector negative,
14 degrees.

219
00:19:02,225 --> 00:19:03,392
1-4 degrees.

220
00:19:07,105 --> 00:19:10,441
We have lock on Event Horizon's
navigation beacon.

221
00:19:11,109 --> 00:19:12,693
She's in the upper ionosphere

222
00:19:12,777 --> 00:19:15,946
and it looks like we're in
for some very heavy chop, sir.

223
00:19:19,659 --> 00:19:22,619
- Bring us in nice and tight, Mr Smith.
- Sir.

224
00:19:22,704 --> 00:19:26,540
- Mr Justin, how's my ship?
- Smooth sailing, sir.

225
00:19:26,833 --> 00:19:28,751
Matching speed on my mark.

226
00:19:28,835 --> 00:19:33,213
Three, two, one. Mark.

227
00:19:37,093 --> 00:19:40,262
- Range?
- 10,000 metres and closing, sir.

228
00:19:40,347 --> 00:19:41,430
- Starck.
- Yes, sir.

229
00:19:41,514 --> 00:19:43,432
Get on the horn.
See if anybody is listening.

230
00:19:43,516 --> 00:19:45,517
This is the US Aerospace
command vessel

231
00:19:45,602 --> 00:19:49,605
Lewis & Clark hailing Event Horizon.
Event Horizon, do you read?

232
00:19:49,689 --> 00:19:50,981
Dr Weir.

233
00:19:52,442 --> 00:19:55,611
- I think you'll want to see this.
- Where is she?

234
00:19:55,695 --> 00:19:57,654
Dead ahead. 5,000 metres!

235
00:19:57,739 --> 00:20:00,866
- Starck? Anybody home?
- Event Horizon, do you read?

236
00:20:00,950 --> 00:20:02,993
3,000 metres and closing!

237
00:20:07,665 --> 00:20:08,749
I can't see a thing.

238
00:20:08,833 --> 00:20:11,502
1,500 metres, sir.
We're getting too close.

239
00:20:11,586 --> 00:20:14,713
- Where is it?
- The scope is lit. It's right in front of us.

240
00:20:14,798 --> 00:20:17,383
- 1,000 metres.
- Proximity warning!

241
00:20:17,467 --> 00:20:21,136
900, 800, 700.

242
00:20:21,221 --> 00:20:23,764
- We're right on top of her, sir!
- We're gonna hit.

243
00:20:23,848 --> 00:20:26,642
- Starck!
- It's right there! God!

244
00:20:28,603 --> 00:20:30,646
Reverse thrusters. Full!

245
00:20:43,910 --> 00:20:45,411
There she is.

246
00:21:05,723 --> 00:21:07,015
Jesus.

247
00:21:07,892 --> 00:21:10,227
Very impressive ship, Doctor.

248
00:21:10,979 --> 00:21:14,356
- Thank you.
- Mr Smith, are you up for a flyby?

249
00:21:15,066 --> 00:21:16,775
Yes, sir. Yes, I'd love to.

250
00:21:31,458 --> 00:21:34,042
That's the main airlock,
we can dock in there.

251
00:21:34,127 --> 00:21:35,961
All right, Smith.

252
00:21:36,045 --> 00:21:39,548
Use the arm and lock us
onto that small antenna cluster.

253
00:21:46,973 --> 00:21:50,767
We should go very carefully here.
That's not a load-bearing structure.

254
00:21:52,520 --> 00:21:54,605
Is now, Doctor.

255
00:21:57,442 --> 00:22:00,694
- Everything five by five?
- Locked in to the Event Horizon.

256
00:22:00,778 --> 00:22:02,905
Thank you, Smitty.
Light them if you got them.

257
00:22:02,989 --> 00:22:05,240
Thank you very much, sir.

258
00:22:06,409 --> 00:22:09,578
- Starck, give me a read.
- The reactor's still hot.

259
00:22:09,662 --> 00:22:11,872
We've got several
small radiation sources,

260
00:22:11,956 --> 00:22:13,999
but no leaks.
Probably it's nothing serious.

261
00:22:14,083 --> 00:22:15,584
- Do they have pressure?
- Affirmative.

262
00:22:15,668 --> 00:22:20,422
The hull's intact, but there's no gravity,
and the thermal units are off-line.

263
00:22:20,507 --> 00:22:22,216
I'm showing deep cold.

264
00:22:22,300 --> 00:22:24,593
The crew couldn't survive
unless they were in stasis.

265
00:22:24,677 --> 00:22:26,970
- Find them, Starck.
- Yeah, I'm already on it, Captain.

266
00:22:27,055 --> 00:22:28,722
Bio-scan on the line.

267
00:22:32,018 --> 00:22:33,352
There's something wrong with the scan.

268
00:22:33,436 --> 00:22:36,563
- Is it radiation interference?
- There's not enough to throw it off.

269
00:22:36,648 --> 00:22:40,526
I'm picking up trace life forms,
but I can't get a lock on their location.

270
00:22:40,610 --> 00:22:43,487
These readings are all over the ship.
It doesn't make any sense.

271
00:22:43,571 --> 00:22:46,990
Okay, we do it the hard way.
Deck by deck, room by room.

272
00:22:47,075 --> 00:22:48,700
Starck, deploy the umbilicus.

273
00:22:48,785 --> 00:22:50,661
Smitty, fire up the boards
in the war room.

274
00:22:50,745 --> 00:22:52,496
Sir.

275
00:22:52,580 --> 00:22:54,706
Mr Justin,
I believe you're up for a walk?

276
00:22:54,791 --> 00:22:56,166
Yes, sir.

277
00:23:00,255 --> 00:23:02,214
Doctor, I'm gonna need you here
on the bridge.

278
00:23:02,298 --> 00:23:03,632
Captain,

279
00:23:03,800 --> 00:23:06,218
I didn't come out all this way
just to sit on your bridge.

280
00:23:06,302 --> 00:23:08,554
- I need to be on that ship.
- Once we've secured the ship,

281
00:23:08,638 --> 00:23:10,556
- we'll bring you on board.
- No, I'm sorry, that's unacceptable.

282
00:23:10,640 --> 00:23:13,183
- I need to be on that ship.
- Once we've secured the ship.

283
00:23:13,268 --> 00:23:14,434
That's the way it is.

284
00:23:14,519 --> 00:23:16,311
I need you to guide us
from the com station.

285
00:23:16,396 --> 00:23:19,439
This is where I need you.
Help us do our job.

286
00:23:32,370 --> 00:23:35,789
(EXCLAIMS) Honey, honey,
you forgot your briefcase.

287
00:23:47,176 --> 00:23:48,594
Stand clear.

288
00:23:50,847 --> 00:23:54,141
- Anything gets funky, Cooper...
- I'll be on it.

289
00:23:55,852 --> 00:23:58,937
Yo, Baby Bear, keep your nose clean.

290
00:24:06,362 --> 00:24:08,030
D.J., showtime.

291
00:24:10,491 --> 00:24:12,451
Video feed is clear.

292
00:24:12,535 --> 00:24:16,121
- Are you with us, Dr Weir?
- Yes, I'm with you.

293
00:24:16,873 --> 00:24:18,624
We are crossing the umbilicus,

294
00:24:18,708 --> 00:24:21,627
<i>making our approach
to the Event Horizon.</i>

295
00:24:23,838 --> 00:24:26,340
You're at the outer airlock door.

296
00:24:27,967 --> 00:24:29,509
Magnetic boots on.

297
00:24:35,350 --> 00:24:37,059
We've got pressure.

298
00:24:56,663 --> 00:24:58,705
Place is a deep freeze.

299
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:03,418
We got ice crystals everywhere.

300
00:25:04,754 --> 00:25:07,005
That's the central corridor
that you're in now.

301
00:25:07,090 --> 00:25:09,675
It connects the personnel areas
at the front of the ship

302
00:25:09,759 --> 00:25:11,343
to Engineering at the rear.

303
00:25:13,596 --> 00:25:16,264
- Mr Justin, you take Engineering.
- Yes, sir.

304
00:25:16,391 --> 00:25:19,351
Peters and I'll take the forward decks.

305
00:25:29,278 --> 00:25:31,279
Doctor, what are these?

306
00:25:32,240 --> 00:25:35,701
Yeah, I've got another one over here.
They are all over the place.

307
00:25:35,785 --> 00:25:36,868
In an emergency,

308
00:25:36,953 --> 00:25:40,455
they destroy the central corridor
and split the ship in half

309
00:25:41,624 --> 00:25:44,626
and then the crew can use
the foredecks as a lifeboat.

310
00:25:50,258 --> 00:25:53,885
CAPTAIN: I'm in Medical. No casualties.

311
00:25:57,932 --> 00:26:00,142
Place looks like it's never been used.

312
00:26:00,226 --> 00:26:01,893
<i>WEIR: You still haven't seen any crew?</i>

313
00:26:01,978 --> 00:26:04,771
If we saw any crew, Doctor,
you'd know it by now.

314
00:26:05,982 --> 00:26:08,150
Scanning for life readings.

315
00:26:11,446 --> 00:26:13,488
This place is a tomb.

316
00:26:16,659 --> 00:26:18,118
Fuck me!

317
00:26:18,995 --> 00:26:20,996
<i>STARCK: Miller. Miller, are you okay?</i>

318
00:26:21,414 --> 00:26:23,749
Looks like Skipper got a case
of the willies.

319
00:26:23,833 --> 00:26:25,917
Cooper, get back to your post.

320
00:26:26,169 --> 00:26:28,420
<i>STARCK: Miller, your heart rate
just went through the roof.</i>

321
00:26:28,504 --> 00:26:30,005
I'm fine.

322
00:26:36,512 --> 00:26:40,098
JUSTIN: I think I've reached the door
to the first containment, Dr Weir.

323
00:26:40,266 --> 00:26:42,434
<i>WEIR: The Engineering decks
are on the other side.</i>

324
00:26:42,518 --> 00:26:44,644
Okay, let's take a look.

325
00:27:01,996 --> 00:27:05,123
What the hell is this place, Dr Weir?

326
00:27:05,208 --> 00:27:07,292
<i>WEIR: It allows you to enter
the second containment</i>

327
00:27:07,376 --> 00:27:10,045
<i>without compromising
the magnetic fields.</i>

328
00:27:11,714 --> 00:27:14,216
Looks like a meat grinder to me.

329
00:27:18,346 --> 00:27:20,055
PETERS: I'm on the bridge.

330
00:27:27,855 --> 00:27:30,065
Got some blood here.

331
00:27:43,746 --> 00:27:48,041
There must have been a coolant leak.
Man, this shit is everywhere.

332
00:27:49,961 --> 00:27:53,797
Coolant level's on reserve,
but well within the safety line.

333
00:28:05,434 --> 00:28:08,144
Miss Peters,
could you turn back and left?

334
00:28:09,939 --> 00:28:12,732
- What is it?
- Ship's log.

335
00:28:32,420 --> 00:28:33,962
It's stuck.

336
00:28:35,923 --> 00:28:38,758
It's pretty jammed in there.

337
00:28:42,346 --> 00:28:44,139
(SCREAMING IN ALARM)

338
00:28:47,018 --> 00:28:49,436
What the hell happened to his eyes?

339
00:28:49,520 --> 00:28:53,356
- Explosive decompression.
- Decompression doesn't do that.

340
00:28:55,026 --> 00:28:58,987
<i>D.J.: Looks like it was caused by...
I don't know, an animal or something.</i>

341
00:28:59,113 --> 00:29:02,616
<i>Look at the damage to the soft tissue,
massive abrasions.</i>

342
00:29:03,159 --> 00:29:04,868
Corpse-sicle.

343
00:29:07,288 --> 00:29:09,539
JUSTIN: I'm scanning for life forms.

344
00:29:14,629 --> 00:29:18,214
Man, I'm getting some
really strange readings in here.

345
00:29:30,186 --> 00:29:33,021
Let me see if I can get some power up.

346
00:29:43,908 --> 00:29:45,867
That's the core, the gravity drive.

347
00:29:52,083 --> 00:29:54,042
The heart of the ship.

348
00:30:09,892 --> 00:30:13,228
Justin. Justin, you're breaking up.

349
00:30:19,360 --> 00:30:20,735
(SCREAMING)

350
00:30:24,031 --> 00:30:25,740
Justin, come in.

351
00:30:37,294 --> 00:30:38,503
- What is it?
- I don't know.

352
00:30:38,587 --> 00:30:40,505
The life scan just went off the scale.

353
00:30:51,642 --> 00:30:53,977
Something's wrong, Starck.
Pull him out. Pull him out.

354
00:30:59,984 --> 00:31:01,359
Shit!

355
00:31:05,781 --> 00:31:06,865
He's in trouble.

356
00:31:06,949 --> 00:31:09,117
- Go, Coop.
- I'm gone.

357
00:31:26,010 --> 00:31:27,719
(MILLER SCREAMING)

358
00:31:47,865 --> 00:31:50,158
Boarding party, sound off.

359
00:31:50,576 --> 00:31:53,244
This is Miller. Can anybody hear me?

360
00:31:59,585 --> 00:32:02,712
- What the fuck was that?
- Pressure emergency!

361
00:32:02,838 --> 00:32:06,091
We've lost the starboard baffle.
The hull's been breached!

362
00:32:07,051 --> 00:32:09,803
I'll contain it here for as long as I can!
Get out!

363
00:32:09,887 --> 00:32:11,930
We're losing atmosphere.

364
00:32:13,516 --> 00:32:16,309
There are pressure suits in the airlock.
Go!

365
00:32:16,393 --> 00:32:18,144
Here I come. Here I come.

366
00:32:22,525 --> 00:32:24,359
Hold on, Baby Bear.

367
00:32:24,693 --> 00:32:26,736
I'm almost there, almost there.

368
00:32:31,534 --> 00:32:33,409
I got you. I got...

369
00:32:34,245 --> 00:32:36,121
Watch out. Watch...

370
00:32:37,414 --> 00:32:40,250
I got you. What the fuck is this?

371
00:32:40,876 --> 00:32:42,127
Hold on.

372
00:32:44,880 --> 00:32:47,882
Captain Miller.
Captain Miller, do you copy? Over.

373
00:32:48,217 --> 00:32:51,970
<i>- Smitty, where you been all my life?
- We have a major situation here, sir.</i>

374
00:32:52,054 --> 00:32:55,473
We've lost the starboard baffle,
and the hull's been breached.

375
00:32:55,641 --> 00:32:58,268
The safety circuits, they have failed.

376
00:32:58,394 --> 00:33:00,186
<i>- Do we have time for a weld?
- No, sir.</i>

377
00:33:00,271 --> 00:33:04,357
We've got 218 litres of gas left, sir,
and then we've got nothing.

378
00:33:04,441 --> 00:33:06,985
The oxygen tanks, they failed,
they're broken.

379
00:33:07,069 --> 00:33:10,321
- Sir, we are fucking dead!
- What about the Event Horizon?

380
00:33:10,823 --> 00:33:12,073
What?

381
00:33:12,158 --> 00:33:14,993
- She's got air and power.
- It's our only chance.

382
00:33:15,077 --> 00:33:17,203
There's no way
I'm getting on that bastard.

383
00:33:17,288 --> 00:33:18,997
We don't even know
what happened on that ship.

384
00:33:19,081 --> 00:33:20,915
It beats dying, Mr Smith.

385
00:33:21,292 --> 00:33:25,086
I want all personnel on board
the Event Horizon, pronto.

386
00:33:25,588 --> 00:33:28,173
- We'll meet at the air tanks.
- I'm ahead of you, sir.

387
00:33:28,257 --> 00:33:31,134
Bringing all thermal units online.

388
00:33:31,260 --> 00:33:34,137
Hold tight. Prep for G's.

389
00:33:34,430 --> 00:33:36,514
Gravity in five seconds.

390
00:33:51,614 --> 00:33:54,824
Wake up, open your eyes. All right?
Just hang in there.

391
00:34:18,974 --> 00:34:20,350
Dr Weir.

392
00:34:21,894 --> 00:34:23,186
Well,

393
00:34:23,771 --> 00:34:26,147
I brought most primary systems online,

394
00:34:26,857 --> 00:34:28,107
- for now.
- Thank you.

395
00:34:28,192 --> 00:34:31,986
- Starck, what's our status?
- The antenna array is completely fried.

396
00:34:32,071 --> 00:34:35,281
We've got no radio,
no laser, no high gain.

397
00:34:35,366 --> 00:34:37,200
No one's coming to help us.

398
00:34:37,743 --> 00:34:40,286
- This air tastes bad.
- Yeah, carbon dioxide, I know.

399
00:34:40,371 --> 00:34:42,664
It's building up
with every breath we take.

400
00:34:42,748 --> 00:34:45,291
And the CO2 filters
on the Event Horizon are shot.

401
00:34:45,376 --> 00:34:46,918
So, we take the scrubbers
from the Clark.

402
00:34:47,002 --> 00:34:50,004
- That gives us what?
- Enough breathable air for 20 hours,

403
00:34:50,089 --> 00:34:52,257
but after that,
we'd better be on our way home.

404
00:35:08,607 --> 00:35:12,277
SMITH: I'm on the port side of the hull,
approaching the dorsal heat sink.

405
00:35:16,323 --> 00:35:17,699
Holy shit.

406
00:35:23,789 --> 00:35:26,249
Captain Miller. Captain Miller,
do you copy? Over.

407
00:35:26,333 --> 00:35:28,710
I copy, Mr Smith. How's my ship?

408
00:35:28,794 --> 00:35:31,671
Sir, we've got a 7-metre fracture
on the outer hull.

409
00:35:31,755 --> 00:35:35,049
<i>We should be able to repair her,
but it'll take an awful lot of time, sir.</i>

410
00:35:35,134 --> 00:35:37,719
Negative. 20 hours, we run out of air.

411
00:35:37,803 --> 00:35:39,137
Understood, sir.

412
00:35:41,765 --> 00:35:43,599
What happened here, Doctor?

413
00:35:55,070 --> 00:35:57,488
Okay, how is he?

414
00:35:58,073 --> 00:36:02,118
His vital signs seem stable,
but he's unresponsive to stimuli.

415
00:36:02,786 --> 00:36:04,037
I don't know.

416
00:36:08,625 --> 00:36:10,585
He might wake up in 15 minutes.

417
00:36:12,588 --> 00:36:14,422
He might not wake up at all.

418
00:36:15,215 --> 00:36:17,258
Look, Skipper,
I know this shit sounds weird,

419
00:36:17,343 --> 00:36:21,804
but, I mean, there was like nothing,
and then Justin appeared,

420
00:36:21,889 --> 00:36:26,517
and it was liquid,
and the whole core just turned solid.

421
00:36:26,602 --> 00:36:28,269
It's just not physically possible.

422
00:36:28,354 --> 00:36:31,272
Doctor, please. Please,
don't start in with that physics shit.

423
00:36:31,357 --> 00:36:33,358
I'm telling you, I saw it.

424
00:36:33,442 --> 00:36:35,943
Mr Cooper,
it would mean the gateway was open.

425
00:36:36,028 --> 00:36:39,822
- That's it. The gateway was open.
- The gateway couldn't be open

426
00:36:39,907 --> 00:36:42,325
because the gravity drive
wasn't activated.

427
00:36:42,409 --> 00:36:44,202
Look, I'm telling you what I saw, Doc!
So...

428
00:36:44,286 --> 00:36:48,623
- It can't just start up by itself.
- Mr Cooper, you are out of line.

429
00:36:49,750 --> 00:36:52,919
Doctor, Mr Justin may die.
Now, I have to assume

430
00:36:53,003 --> 00:36:56,381
that whatever happened to him
could happen to all of us.

431
00:36:56,465 --> 00:37:01,177
Mr Cooper says he saw something.
I believe he saw something.

432
00:37:02,054 --> 00:37:04,305
I need an explanation.

433
00:37:07,851 --> 00:37:12,021
In my view, Mr Cooper's delusional,
if you don't mind my saying so.

434
00:37:12,106 --> 00:37:15,441
But maybe he saw an optical effect

435
00:37:15,526 --> 00:37:17,360
caused by gravitational distortion.

436
00:37:17,444 --> 00:37:19,529
"An optical effect"?
That's fucking poetic.

437
00:37:19,613 --> 00:37:21,489
- Where the fuck...
- Cooper!

438
00:37:21,573 --> 00:37:23,699
I'm sorry. All right.

439
00:37:25,828 --> 00:37:28,955
Gravitational distortion.
What could create that, Doctor?

440
00:37:29,039 --> 00:37:33,459
If, somehow, a burst of gravity waves
escaped from the core,

441
00:37:34,586 --> 00:37:38,464
it could distort space-time.
It could make Justin seem to disappear.

442
00:37:38,549 --> 00:37:42,009
It could also
have damaged the Lewis & Clark.

443
00:37:43,011 --> 00:37:47,640
- However, I think this is entirely unlikely.
- What's in the core, Doctor?

444
00:37:48,725 --> 00:37:49,892
It's complicated.

445
00:37:49,977 --> 00:37:52,728
How much time do you need?
We have,

446
00:37:52,813 --> 00:37:55,314
17 hours and 48 minutes.

447
00:37:56,567 --> 00:37:58,359
What's in the core?

448
00:38:00,320 --> 00:38:02,822
This is the gateway.

449
00:38:02,906 --> 00:38:05,783
Now, these three magnetic rings,
when they align,

450
00:38:05,868 --> 00:38:08,119
it creates an artificial black hole

451
00:38:08,203 --> 00:38:10,913
which allows the ship to travel
to any point in space.

452
00:38:10,998 --> 00:38:14,792
A black hole, the most destructive force
in the universe.

453
00:38:15,210 --> 00:38:17,753
- And you've created one?
- Absolutely, yes.

454
00:38:17,838 --> 00:38:22,925
Because we can use that
immense power to bend space-time.

455
00:38:23,010 --> 00:38:26,596
Look, it would take the Lewis & Clark
1,000 years

456
00:38:26,680 --> 00:38:28,931
to reach our nearest star.

457
00:38:29,016 --> 00:38:31,476
But the Event Horizon
could be there in a day.

458
00:38:31,560 --> 00:38:34,729
- If it worked.
- You can come down. It's perfectly safe.

459
00:38:40,444 --> 00:38:43,404
So, if Mr Justin was sucked
through your gateway,

460
00:38:43,489 --> 00:38:45,990
he could've gone wherever
the Event Horizon's been.

461
00:38:46,074 --> 00:38:48,534
Theoretically, yes.
But as I told you before,

462
00:38:48,619 --> 00:38:52,497
- the gateway can't just open by itself.
- I see.

463
00:38:52,581 --> 00:38:54,290
Lieutenant, I want this room sealed off.

464
00:38:54,374 --> 00:38:56,250
Second containment from here on in
is off-limits.

465
00:38:56,335 --> 00:38:58,961
- Yes, sir.
- Captain, there's no danger.

466
00:38:59,046 --> 00:39:01,088
The gateway's
contained behind three magnetic fields.

467
00:39:01,173 --> 00:39:03,758
- It's perfectly safe.
- Safe?

468
00:39:03,842 --> 00:39:05,218
Doctor, my ship is in pieces

469
00:39:05,302 --> 00:39:08,012
and one of my crewmen
may not make it home alive.

470
00:39:08,096 --> 00:39:10,598
No one else goes near this thing.

471
00:39:31,161 --> 00:39:32,453
Come on.

472
00:39:34,540 --> 00:39:37,458
There's got to be something on this log.

473
00:39:38,544 --> 00:39:40,211
You can do this.

474
00:39:40,754 --> 00:39:42,380
(WHOOSHING)

475
00:39:44,132 --> 00:39:45,341
D.J.?

476
00:39:52,558 --> 00:39:54,183
(WHOOSHING)

477
00:40:05,529 --> 00:40:09,031
<i>- D.J., where are you?
- L'm up on Deck 4.</i>

478
00:40:41,607 --> 00:40:43,232
(WHOOSHING)

479
00:41:02,836 --> 00:41:04,045
Mommy.

480
00:41:16,892 --> 00:41:18,142
Peters!

481
00:41:24,566 --> 00:41:26,734
What is it? You all right?

482
00:41:43,752 --> 00:41:46,003
What's up? You was out there so long,

483
00:41:46,088 --> 00:41:47,838
I thought you were
trying to break my record.

484
00:41:47,923 --> 00:41:51,175
Listen, I'd rather spend the next
12 hours outside

485
00:41:51,259 --> 00:41:53,636
than another five seconds
in this shit can.

486
00:41:53,720 --> 00:41:56,097
It's the final entry in the ship's log.

487
00:41:56,598 --> 00:41:59,433
<i>KILPACK: I want to say how proud I am
of my crew.</i>

488
00:41:59,559 --> 00:42:01,686
<i>L'd like to name my station heads:</i>

489
00:42:01,770 --> 00:42:03,854
<i>Chris Chambers, Janice Reuben,</i>

490
00:42:04,564 --> 00:42:07,024
<i>Ben Fender, Dick Smith.</i>

491
00:42:09,236 --> 00:42:10,653
<i>We've reached safe distance...</i>

492
00:42:10,737 --> 00:42:12,446
This is John Kilpack, ship's captain.

493
00:42:12,531 --> 00:42:15,950
<i>...and we're preparing to engage
the gravity drive,</i>

494
00:42:16,076 --> 00:42:18,953
<i>and open the gateway
to Proxima Centauri.</i>

495
00:42:19,788 --> 00:42:21,455
(SPEAKING LATIN)

496
00:42:22,624 --> 00:42:24,041
<i>Hail and farewell.</i>

497
00:42:25,127 --> 00:42:26,961
(STATIC INTERFERENCE)

498
00:42:32,551 --> 00:42:34,719
- What is that?
- I'll run it through a few filters,

499
00:42:34,803 --> 00:42:36,929
see if I can clean it up.

500
00:42:40,934 --> 00:42:43,477
- It's a power drain.
- It's the core.

501
00:42:44,980 --> 00:42:46,731
Stay here. Look after Justin.

502
00:42:46,815 --> 00:42:48,649
I don't want anybody else
near that thing.

503
00:42:48,734 --> 00:42:49,942
Wait for me, Doctor.

504
00:42:51,653 --> 00:42:55,406
- What's causing the drain?
- Magnetic fields are holding.

505
00:42:55,490 --> 00:42:58,743
Maybe a short in the fail-safe circuit.
I'll check it.

506
00:42:58,827 --> 00:43:00,411
Can you give us a hand?

507
00:43:21,349 --> 00:43:22,641
Justin.

508
00:43:23,018 --> 00:43:24,685
Justin, can you hear me?

509
00:43:25,645 --> 00:43:27,813
- He's coming.
- Who's coming?

510
00:43:29,858 --> 00:43:31,650
The dark.

511
00:43:32,903 --> 00:43:34,320
(SCREAMING)

512
00:43:48,668 --> 00:43:50,878
Yeah, that's the one.

513
00:44:04,184 --> 00:44:05,392
<i>CLAIRE: Billy.</i>

514
00:44:16,696 --> 00:44:17,905
<i>Billy.</i>

515
00:44:33,213 --> 00:44:34,922
<i>Billy.</i>

516
00:44:45,392 --> 00:44:46,642
Miller!

517
00:44:47,602 --> 00:44:49,436
Captain Miller!

518
00:44:49,563 --> 00:44:51,772
I've got some problems here.

519
00:44:56,945 --> 00:44:58,445
Be with me,

520
00:44:59,447 --> 00:45:00,739
forever.

521
00:45:09,165 --> 00:45:10,499
Dr Weir?

522
00:45:37,319 --> 00:45:40,237
Carbon dioxide can produce
hallucinations, impair judgement.

523
00:45:40,322 --> 00:45:42,406
God damn it, D.J.,
it was not a hallucination.

524
00:45:42,490 --> 00:45:43,866
All right.

525
00:45:44,034 --> 00:45:46,327
Doctor, you were in the duct.

526
00:45:46,536 --> 00:45:48,954
You must have heard something.

527
00:45:50,165 --> 00:45:52,833
- You must have seen something.
- I did.

528
00:45:53,001 --> 00:45:55,586
About an hour ago, I saw my son

529
00:45:55,670 --> 00:45:58,213
lying on the examination table

530
00:46:01,468 --> 00:46:02,968
- and in his legs were...
- Miss Peters...

531
00:46:03,053 --> 00:46:04,887
- ... crawling...
- ... if you don't mind my saying so,

532
00:46:04,971 --> 00:46:06,221
it sounds a little like shock.

533
00:46:06,306 --> 00:46:08,140
- Could it be that you're traumatised...
- No. No.

534
00:46:08,224 --> 00:46:10,893
- ... by seeing the body on the...
- Excuse me. No!

535
00:46:12,520 --> 00:46:15,105
I've seen bodies before.
This was different.

536
00:46:15,190 --> 00:46:17,107
She's right. Now, this was real.

537
00:46:17,192 --> 00:46:19,860
I felt... I felt heat.

538
00:46:19,986 --> 00:46:22,863
This is not something
that's in our heads.

539
00:46:24,699 --> 00:46:27,993
Smitty, have you seen anything unusual
at all?

540
00:46:28,536 --> 00:46:31,080
No, I haven't seen anything,
and I don't need to see anything, sir,

541
00:46:31,164 --> 00:46:33,332
but I can tell you

542
00:46:33,416 --> 00:46:35,376
this ship is fucked.

543
00:46:35,502 --> 00:46:38,128
Thank you for that scientific analysis,
Mr Smith.

544
00:46:38,213 --> 00:46:40,297
Well, you don't expect
to figure it out, do you?

545
00:46:40,382 --> 00:46:41,340
All right, Doc.

546
00:46:41,424 --> 00:46:42,633
When you break
all the laws of physics,

547
00:46:42,717 --> 00:46:44,134
you seriously think
there won't be a price?

548
00:46:44,219 --> 00:46:47,471
You already killed the last fucking crew.
And now you want to kill us as well!

549
00:46:47,555 --> 00:46:50,015
- It's just a fucking ship, all right?
- D.J.!

550
00:46:50,100 --> 00:46:53,185
It's just a ship, do you understand?
It's just a big hunk of metal.

551
00:46:53,269 --> 00:46:56,063
- There's nothing odd going on.
- D.J.?

552
00:47:00,068 --> 00:47:02,194
- Fine.
- D.J.?

553
00:47:05,615 --> 00:47:08,033
- You all right?
- Yeah, I'm just...

554
00:47:08,743 --> 00:47:10,077
I'm sorry.

555
00:47:10,245 --> 00:47:11,787
- Damn it!
- Smitty.

556
00:47:11,913 --> 00:47:13,998
- It's all right. That's it.
- Fucking...

557
00:47:14,082 --> 00:47:17,584
That's enough! I want you calm,
I want you cool! Now!

558
00:47:19,421 --> 00:47:22,172
- All right.
- You're going back upstairs.

559
00:47:22,257 --> 00:47:24,383
You're going outside on the Clark
with Cooper,

560
00:47:24,467 --> 00:47:26,885
and you're going to repair the ship.
No mistakes.

561
00:47:26,970 --> 00:47:29,763
Mistakes, nobody goes home.
Understood?

562
00:47:30,598 --> 00:47:32,474
- Yes, sir.
- All right.

563
00:47:33,143 --> 00:47:34,893
- Sir.
- Get moving.

564
00:47:41,985 --> 00:47:43,694
- Miller.
- What is it, Starck?

565
00:47:43,778 --> 00:47:46,488
I ran the bio-scan
with the DNA-RNA filters.

566
00:47:46,614 --> 00:47:47,823
The results were bio readings...

567
00:47:47,907 --> 00:47:49,533
Of an indeterminate origin. Yes, I know.

568
00:47:49,617 --> 00:47:52,369
Don't you have anything useful
to tell me, Lieutenant?

569
00:47:52,454 --> 00:47:55,247
- I've got a theory.
- Let's hear it.

570
00:47:55,331 --> 00:47:58,834
I think that there's a connection between
the readings and the hallucinations,

571
00:47:58,918 --> 00:48:01,253
like they're all part
of a defensive reaction,

572
00:48:01,337 --> 00:48:02,588
some sort of immune system.

573
00:48:02,672 --> 00:48:04,256
I don't have time to listen to this.

574
00:48:04,340 --> 00:48:05,799
- You have to listen to me.
- To what?

575
00:48:05,884 --> 00:48:09,386
I'm saying that this ship is reacting to us
and the reactions are getting stronger.

576
00:48:09,471 --> 00:48:11,346
It's as if the ship
brought back something with it,

577
00:48:11,431 --> 00:48:13,015
a life force of some kind.

578
00:48:13,099 --> 00:48:15,726
What are you telling me?
That this ship is alive?

579
00:48:15,810 --> 00:48:18,520
You wanted an answer,
and it's the only one I've got.

580
00:48:18,605 --> 00:48:22,107
No, what I want, Lieutenant,
is to survive the next 10 hours.

581
00:49:06,986 --> 00:49:08,237
Justin?

582
00:49:09,572 --> 00:49:10,864
Justin!

583
00:49:14,536 --> 00:49:15,661
Justin!

584
00:49:15,745 --> 00:49:17,246
(LOUD BANGING)

585
00:50:06,921 --> 00:50:09,131
Did you hear that? Did you hear that?

586
00:50:09,215 --> 00:50:10,507
- What's that?
- What is it?

587
00:50:10,592 --> 00:50:12,009
- You heard it.
- What did you hear?

588
00:50:12,093 --> 00:50:13,510
- What is it?
- Peters, listen to me.

589
00:50:13,595 --> 00:50:14,887
It's D.J., all right?

590
00:50:14,971 --> 00:50:16,972
I didn't hear anything.
Neither of us heard anything.

591
00:50:17,056 --> 00:50:18,348
- So just calm down, all right?
- Okay.

592
00:50:18,433 --> 00:50:20,434
- Get your breath back.
- Okay.

593
00:50:20,560 --> 00:50:22,644
- Now, tell me.
- I was...

594
00:50:22,729 --> 00:50:24,229
(LOUD BANGING)

595
00:50:27,066 --> 00:50:29,818
Make it stop! Make it stop!

596
00:50:31,237 --> 00:50:32,779
Open the door.

597
00:50:34,240 --> 00:50:35,741
Stop moving!

598
00:50:38,620 --> 00:50:41,496
- No!
- Weir, stop. Stop him!

599
00:50:41,623 --> 00:50:43,457
What're you doing?

600
00:50:46,461 --> 00:50:50,631
In our current environment, Dr Weir,
self-control is an asset.

601
00:50:54,802 --> 00:50:56,386
<i>(ALARM BLARING)</i>

602
00:50:56,846 --> 00:50:58,305
What is it?

603
00:50:59,182 --> 00:51:01,016
The forward airlock.

604
00:51:01,309 --> 00:51:04,102
Miller, Smith, Cooper,
anyone in the airlock?

605
00:51:04,187 --> 00:51:05,979
<i>MILLER: That's a negative, Starck.</i>

606
00:51:06,648 --> 00:51:07,856
Justin.

607
00:51:10,652 --> 00:51:12,319
- Justin.
- Don't!

608
00:51:12,445 --> 00:51:13,987
Justin, no!

609
00:51:14,113 --> 00:51:16,949
Justin, no! Justin, open the door.

610
00:51:18,451 --> 00:51:19,868
Miller, come in.

611
00:51:19,994 --> 00:51:22,829
<i>Miller, come in. We have an emergency.</i>

612
00:51:22,956 --> 00:51:24,790
What's going on in there, Starck?

613
00:51:24,874 --> 00:51:27,417
- Justin's in the airlock.
- Say again.

614
00:51:27,502 --> 00:51:30,379
Justin is in the airlock
and he is not wearing a suit.

615
00:51:30,463 --> 00:51:32,839
- Shit. I'm on it.
- As you were, sailor.

616
00:51:32,966 --> 00:51:35,467
- Skipper, you need me on this.
- I need you right where you are.

617
00:51:35,551 --> 00:51:37,386
He's engaged the override.

618
00:51:37,512 --> 00:51:39,513
- Can you shut it down?
- I'll try.

619
00:51:40,014 --> 00:51:41,265
Coming to him, Starck.

620
00:51:41,349 --> 00:51:44,226
Justin! Open this door, now!

621
00:51:47,981 --> 00:51:49,314
Starck, give me status.

622
00:51:49,399 --> 00:51:51,316
You better hurry.
He's engaged the override

623
00:51:51,401 --> 00:51:53,610
- and we can't open the inner door.
- I'll get the kit.

624
00:51:53,695 --> 00:51:56,488
Justin, open the door!

625
00:52:00,868 --> 00:52:04,037
Justin, open the door.

626
00:52:04,163 --> 00:52:06,456
- Did you hear it?
- Keep him talking.

627
00:52:06,541 --> 00:52:09,960
Yes. Yes, I heard it.
Do you know what it is?

628
00:52:10,044 --> 00:52:12,045
It shows you things,

629
00:52:12,171 --> 00:52:15,382
- horrible things.
- What does?

630
00:52:15,466 --> 00:52:19,720
The dark inside me
from the other place.

631
00:52:19,804 --> 00:52:22,973
- I won't go back there. I won't.
- No, Baby Bear.

632
00:52:23,057 --> 00:52:24,808
- Come on, open the door.
- What's happening?

633
00:52:24,892 --> 00:52:27,144
- I don't think she can talk him down.
- She'd better.

634
00:52:27,228 --> 00:52:29,438
If he opens the airlock,
he's going to turn inside out.

635
00:52:29,522 --> 00:52:31,982
- No. You stay with me.
- Almost got it.

636
00:52:32,066 --> 00:52:34,443
- I've almost got it.
- You stay with me, Justin.

637
00:52:34,527 --> 00:52:36,153
Come on, now. Open that door!

638
00:52:36,237 --> 00:52:39,656
If you could see the things I've seen,
you wouldn't try to stop me.

639
00:52:39,741 --> 00:52:41,950
No, that's not you talking.
That's not you talking.

640
00:52:42,035 --> 00:52:44,077
That's it. That's it, right there.

641
00:52:44,162 --> 00:52:46,830
Open the door. Go for that button.
Come on.

642
00:52:46,914 --> 00:52:48,081
Okay.

643
00:52:50,084 --> 00:52:51,084
No!

644
00:52:53,421 --> 00:52:56,923
Captain, Justin just activated the door.
It's on a 30-second delay.

645
00:52:57,008 --> 00:52:59,092
Justin!

646
00:53:02,722 --> 00:53:06,433
<i>WOMAN OVER PA: Stand by
for decompression in 25 seconds.</i>

647
00:53:07,435 --> 00:53:09,353
Where am I?

648
00:53:09,896 --> 00:53:11,605
- Hey.
- Starck, get it fucking open!

649
00:53:11,689 --> 00:53:12,939
- Hey, open the door.
- I can't.

650
00:53:13,024 --> 00:53:15,525
The inner door won't open
when the outside door's been triggered.

651
00:53:15,610 --> 00:53:17,694
- It'd decompress the entire ship.
- Oh, my God.

652
00:53:17,779 --> 00:53:20,614
Mama Bear, open the door.

653
00:53:20,740 --> 00:53:22,407
Come on, please.

654
00:53:23,034 --> 00:53:25,369
<i>- Captain!
- Patch me through to him.</i>

655
00:53:25,453 --> 00:53:27,204
Captain Miller, tell them
to open the door.

656
00:53:27,288 --> 00:53:29,122
They can't do that, Justin.

657
00:53:29,207 --> 00:53:32,042
- I don't want to die in here.
- You're not gonna die.

658
00:53:32,126 --> 00:53:36,338
I want you to listen to me very carefully,
and I'm gonna get you out of there.

659
00:53:39,467 --> 00:53:40,926
Oh, my God.

660
00:53:41,260 --> 00:53:42,803
It's starting.

661
00:53:43,221 --> 00:53:45,722
<i>- My eyes.
- Don't think about them. Shut them.</i>

662
00:53:45,807 --> 00:53:47,974
<i>Just shut them as tight as you can.</i>

663
00:53:48,101 --> 00:53:49,184
Five seconds.

664
00:53:49,268 --> 00:53:52,562
Tuck yourself into a crouch position.
Make yourself into a ball now.

665
00:53:52,647 --> 00:53:55,732
<i>JUSTIN: I can't breathe. Oh, God.</i>

666
00:53:55,983 --> 00:53:58,235
<i>Oh, God, it hurts.</i>

667
00:53:58,319 --> 00:54:02,572
I want you to huff and puff
and blow all the air out of your lungs.

668
00:54:02,657 --> 00:54:05,659
Do you hear me?
All the air out of your lungs, Justin.

669
00:54:05,743 --> 00:54:07,244
(JUSTIN SCREAMING)

670
00:54:07,328 --> 00:54:08,995
Do it, now!

671
00:54:21,008 --> 00:54:22,676
Okay, Baby Bear.

672
00:54:24,512 --> 00:54:28,515
Got him. I got him. Stand by, people.

673
00:54:28,599 --> 00:54:29,933
Stand by.

674
00:54:31,978 --> 00:54:35,439
- We've got pressure.
- We're coming, Justin. We're coming.

675
00:54:37,108 --> 00:54:40,652
I'm going to need five units here.
Hold his head still.

676
00:54:41,446 --> 00:54:42,654
Okay.

677
00:54:44,699 --> 00:54:47,033
Give me some more glycerin, now.

678
00:54:47,118 --> 00:54:48,702
<i>D.J.: One thing at a time,
let's keep him breathing.</i>

679
00:54:48,786 --> 00:54:49,911
<i>STARCK: Christ, he's bleeding out.</i>

680
00:54:49,996 --> 00:54:52,873
<i>- Pressure's still dropping.
- Start the drip, 15 cc.</i>

681
00:55:01,299 --> 00:55:04,801
I've stopped the bleeding,
stabilised him best I can.

682
00:55:06,220 --> 00:55:09,139
He won't be pretty, but he should live

683
00:55:09,474 --> 00:55:12,058
- if we make it back.
- We'll make it.

684
00:55:13,644 --> 00:55:15,896
Starck, what's our time like?

685
00:55:15,980 --> 00:55:18,899
CO2 levels will become toxic
in four hours.

686
00:55:19,650 --> 00:55:20,901
All right.

687
00:55:20,985 --> 00:55:23,737
Peters, we've got to find out
what happened to the other crew

688
00:55:23,821 --> 00:55:26,490
before the same thing happens to us.

689
00:55:26,574 --> 00:55:30,827
I can work on the log on the bridge,
but I won't go back to Medical.

690
00:55:30,912 --> 00:55:33,580
- Fine.
- Yes, sir.

691
00:55:35,833 --> 00:55:38,210
Justin said something about,

692
00:55:39,420 --> 00:55:41,338
"the dark inside me. "

693
00:55:42,507 --> 00:55:44,257
What's that mean?

694
00:55:45,051 --> 00:55:46,343
Doctor?

695
00:55:48,513 --> 00:55:50,931
I don't think it means anything.

696
00:55:55,269 --> 00:55:57,896
Don't you walk away from me, mister.

697
00:55:59,524 --> 00:56:01,358
I'd like some answers, Doctor.

698
00:56:01,442 --> 00:56:04,027
I'd like to know why one of my men
tried to commit suicide

699
00:56:04,111 --> 00:56:08,281
by throwing himself out of an airlock.
I'd like to know what caused that noise.

700
00:56:08,366 --> 00:56:09,991
Look, thermal changes in the hull

701
00:56:10,076 --> 00:56:13,370
could've caused the metal
to expand and contract rapidly,

702
00:56:13,454 --> 00:56:14,996
causing reverberations.
This is possible.

703
00:56:15,081 --> 00:56:19,125
Bullshit! You built this fucking ship.
You've given me nothing but bullshit.

704
00:56:19,252 --> 00:56:21,461
What do you want me to say?

705
00:56:21,712 --> 00:56:25,549
You said the ship's drive
creates a gateway.

706
00:56:25,633 --> 00:56:27,300
- Yeah.
- To what?

707
00:56:28,761 --> 00:56:30,512
- I don't know.
- Where did it go?

708
00:56:30,596 --> 00:56:33,557
- Where did you send it?
- I don't know.

709
00:56:33,641 --> 00:56:36,059
Where has it been
for the last seven years?

710
00:56:36,143 --> 00:56:37,227
Look, I don't know.

711
00:56:37,311 --> 00:56:39,396
"I don't know,"
is not good enough, Doctor.

712
00:56:39,480 --> 00:56:41,356
You're supposed to be
the fucking expert.

713
00:56:41,440 --> 00:56:43,567
I need answers. That's your job.

714
00:56:43,651 --> 00:56:47,487
- Now, the other place, where is that?
- I don't know! I don't know!

715
00:56:48,322 --> 00:56:49,406
Look,

716
00:56:50,157 --> 00:56:52,200
there's a lot of things
happening around here

717
00:56:52,285 --> 00:56:55,745
that I don't fully understand. I need time.

718
00:56:57,123 --> 00:56:58,331
I see.

719
00:56:58,916 --> 00:57:02,168
Well, that's exactly
what we don't have, Doctor.

720
00:57:07,341 --> 00:57:08,675
<i>CORRICK: Captain!</i>

721
00:57:14,932 --> 00:57:16,516
<i>Don't leave me!</i>

722
00:57:23,024 --> 00:57:24,608
<i>Don't leave me!</i>

723
00:57:30,031 --> 00:57:31,323
<i>Please!</i>

724
00:57:34,619 --> 00:57:38,496
<i>For God's sakes, help me!</i>

725
00:57:48,549 --> 00:57:50,300
It's in your head.

726
00:57:52,887 --> 00:57:54,888
It's just in your head.

727
00:57:58,225 --> 00:58:00,226
(METALLIC REVERBERATIONS)

728
00:58:14,742 --> 00:58:16,201
God help us.

729
00:58:21,999 --> 00:58:24,334
I'm telling you, it was his voice I heard.

730
00:58:24,418 --> 00:58:28,254
He was calling to me.
A young bosun named Eddie Corrick.

731
00:58:30,091 --> 00:58:32,676
We served on the Goliath together.

732
00:58:34,011 --> 00:58:38,181
When the O2 tanks ruptured,
four of us made it to the lifeboat.

733
00:58:38,265 --> 00:58:42,686
But Corrick was still on board
the Goliath when the fire broke out.

734
00:58:43,020 --> 00:58:45,605
You ever seen fire in zero gravity?

735
00:58:47,525 --> 00:58:49,109
It's beautiful.

736
00:58:51,278 --> 00:58:53,029
It's like liquid.

737
00:58:54,115 --> 00:58:56,449
It slides all over everything.

738
00:58:57,618 --> 00:58:59,369
Comes up in waves.

739
00:59:00,913 --> 00:59:03,415
And they just kept hitting him,

740
00:59:03,791 --> 00:59:05,458
wave after wave.

741
00:59:07,712 --> 00:59:10,296
He was screaming for me to save him.

742
00:59:11,298 --> 00:59:12,966
What did you do?

743
00:59:13,467 --> 00:59:15,802
I did the only thing I could.

744
00:59:17,805 --> 00:59:21,224
I closed the lifeboat hatch,
and I left him behind.

745
00:59:25,271 --> 00:59:27,814
I swore I'd never lose another man.

746
00:59:29,108 --> 00:59:31,276
I've known you a long time.

747
00:59:31,819 --> 00:59:36,156
- You never told me that.
- That's just it, D.J., I never told anybody.

748
00:59:37,575 --> 00:59:39,826
But this ship knew about it.

749
00:59:40,661 --> 00:59:44,247
It knows my fears. It knows my secrets.

750
00:59:45,499 --> 00:59:48,293
Gets inside your head,
and it shows you.

751
00:59:49,754 --> 00:59:52,172
I wasn't going to tell you this.

752
00:59:54,133 --> 00:59:57,177
I've been listening to the distress signal.

753
00:59:57,344 --> 00:59:58,845
And I...

754
01:00:00,931 --> 01:00:03,933
I think I made a mistake
in the translation.

755
01:00:05,186 --> 01:00:06,936
<i>(INDISTINCT SPEECH)</i>

756
01:00:12,693 --> 01:00:13,943
Go on.

757
01:00:15,196 --> 01:00:17,530
I thought it said "liberate me. "

758
01:00:18,324 --> 01:00:19,616
Save me.

759
01:00:20,993 --> 01:00:22,660
But it's not "me. "

760
01:00:23,788 --> 01:00:26,206
<i>It's "liberate tutume. "</i>

761
01:00:27,291 --> 01:00:28,833
Save yourself.

762
01:00:29,710 --> 01:00:31,461
And it gets worse.

763
01:00:31,712 --> 01:00:33,463
<i>(INDISTINCT SPEECH)</i>

764
01:00:35,174 --> 01:00:36,382
There.

765
01:00:37,676 --> 01:00:39,052
I think,

766
01:00:39,845 --> 01:00:42,972
<i>that says "ex inferis. "</i>

767
01:00:44,475 --> 01:00:46,142
"Save yourself,

768
01:00:48,562 --> 01:00:49,896
"from hell. "

769
01:00:51,524 --> 01:00:54,901
Look, if what Dr Weir tells us is true,

770
01:00:56,403 --> 01:01:00,240
this ship has been beyond
the boundaries of our universe

771
01:01:00,366 --> 01:01:02,575
of known scientific reality.

772
01:01:03,994 --> 01:01:06,246
Who knows where it's been,

773
01:01:06,705 --> 01:01:08,414
what it's seen,

774
01:01:10,334 --> 01:01:12,919
and what it's brought back with it.

775
01:01:13,254 --> 01:01:14,629
From hell.

776
01:01:18,843 --> 01:01:22,095
You don't believe in that kind of stuff,
do you?

777
01:01:24,849 --> 01:01:28,309
Whoever sent that message,
he sure believes in hell.

778
01:01:29,270 --> 01:01:30,854
<i>COOPER: Captain Miller.</i>

779
01:01:35,025 --> 01:01:37,068
Whatever it is, Coop, it better be good.

780
01:01:37,152 --> 01:01:38,236
<i>Yes, sir.</i>

781
01:01:38,320 --> 01:01:41,072
<i>Ready to pressurise the Clark
and get the hell out of here.</i>

782
01:01:41,156 --> 01:01:42,574
On my way.

783
01:01:47,329 --> 01:01:49,455
Come on, baby, don't let me down.

784
01:01:57,256 --> 01:02:00,216
Hello, baby. Papa's home.

785
01:02:00,843 --> 01:02:02,385
SMITH: She's holding.

786
01:02:03,637 --> 01:02:04,846
Oh, shit.

787
01:02:04,930 --> 01:02:07,849
Skipper, we're still venting
trace gasses.

788
01:02:07,933 --> 01:02:10,018
Give me about 20 minutes
to plug the hole.

789
01:02:10,102 --> 01:02:14,856
<i>Cooper, you are the lifesaver.
20 minutes and we're underway.</i>

790
01:02:18,569 --> 01:02:19,736
Shit.

791
01:02:23,115 --> 01:02:26,784
- Got any coffee?
- Yeah, but it's cold.

792
01:02:26,869 --> 01:02:28,119
Great.

793
01:02:35,961 --> 01:02:37,337
<i>(SCREAMING)</i>

794
01:02:38,464 --> 01:02:39,672
Starck.

795
01:02:45,220 --> 01:02:46,262
Miller?

796
01:02:55,022 --> 01:02:58,775
<i>Liberate tutemet ex inferis.</i>

797
01:03:07,242 --> 01:03:08,534
We're leaving.

798
01:03:11,580 --> 01:03:13,456
No, we can't leave.

799
01:03:14,833 --> 01:03:16,542
Our orders are specific.

800
01:03:16,627 --> 01:03:19,504
Rescue the crew,
salvage what's left of the ship.

801
01:03:19,588 --> 01:03:22,548
The crew is dead, Doctor.
Your ship killed them.

802
01:03:22,633 --> 01:03:25,843
- We came here to do a job.
- We're aborting, Doctor!

803
01:03:26,553 --> 01:03:29,806
Starck, download the files
from the Event Horizon's computers.

804
01:03:29,890 --> 01:03:32,100
D.J., I want you to get Justin prepped

805
01:03:32,184 --> 01:03:33,476
and ready to move him back
to the Clark.

806
01:03:33,560 --> 01:03:36,020
There's stuff I wanna get from medical.
We'd better move him in the tank.

807
01:03:36,105 --> 01:03:37,772
No problem. Do it.

808
01:03:39,692 --> 01:03:40,900
- Peters.
- Yes, sir.

809
01:03:40,985 --> 01:03:43,111
I want you to get the CO2 scrubbers
back into the Clark.

810
01:03:43,195 --> 01:03:45,321
- Take Smitty with you.
- Right.

811
01:03:45,406 --> 01:03:46,739
Captain.

812
01:03:48,534 --> 01:03:49,826
Captain.

813
01:03:51,996 --> 01:03:55,289
- Don't do this.
- It's done.

814
01:03:56,917 --> 01:03:58,960
What about my ship?
You can't just leave her.

815
01:03:59,044 --> 01:04:00,712
I have no intention of leaving her,
Doctor.

816
01:04:00,796 --> 01:04:02,797
I will take the Lewis & Clark
to a safe distance,

817
01:04:02,881 --> 01:04:05,425
<i>and then I will launch tac missiles
at the Event Horizon,</i>

818
01:04:05,509 --> 01:04:09,095
until I'm satisfied she's vapourised.
Fuck this ship.

819
01:04:16,854 --> 01:04:20,231
Captain Miller,
the bio-scan just went off the scale.

820
01:04:20,315 --> 01:04:21,691
It looks like the core

821
01:04:21,775 --> 01:04:24,193
is draining power
from the rest of the ship.

822
01:04:24,278 --> 01:04:27,155
Get the files. Vacate.
I want off this ship.

823
01:04:27,239 --> 01:04:30,700
You can't leave. She won't let you.

824
01:04:30,784 --> 01:04:33,411
You just get your gear and get back
on the Lewis & Clark, Doctor,

825
01:04:33,495 --> 01:04:35,580
or you'll find yourself walking home.

826
01:04:35,664 --> 01:04:37,540
I am home.

827
01:04:38,208 --> 01:04:39,459
Let's go!

828
01:04:40,002 --> 01:04:41,210
Ready?

829
01:04:42,171 --> 01:04:43,755
Shot.

830
01:04:44,882 --> 01:04:46,174
Come on.

831
01:04:47,259 --> 01:04:48,801
- Ready?
- Shot.

832
01:04:54,058 --> 01:04:55,683
- Shot.
- Okay.

833
01:04:56,185 --> 01:04:58,394
Peters, can we go?
This place is really freaking me out.

834
01:04:58,479 --> 01:05:00,104
Yeah, you wanna breathe
on the way home.

835
01:05:00,189 --> 01:05:02,398
- No, I don't! Let's go!
- Shit!

836
01:05:02,941 --> 01:05:04,317
- 25.
- Come on!

837
01:05:04,401 --> 01:05:06,402
- We need 25.
- Peters!

838
01:05:06,487 --> 01:05:08,613
Come on! Got it!

839
01:06:48,839 --> 01:06:50,131
(GASPING)

840
01:06:50,215 --> 01:06:51,591
(SCREAMING)

841
01:07:33,425 --> 01:07:35,343
Oh, no. Peters.

842
01:07:41,433 --> 01:07:43,059
Oh, God.

843
01:07:48,982 --> 01:07:50,024
<i>CLAIRE: Billy.</i>

844
01:08:00,160 --> 01:08:01,410
Claire?

845
01:08:08,919 --> 01:08:09,961
Claire.

846
01:08:11,630 --> 01:08:13,297
It's me. I'm home.

847
01:08:15,801 --> 01:08:17,343
I'm home.

848
01:08:21,265 --> 01:08:24,267
I know I wasn't there
when you needed me,

849
01:08:25,310 --> 01:08:27,186
and I'm sorry I...

850
01:08:27,896 --> 01:08:31,065
I let my work come between us.

851
01:08:35,112 --> 01:08:37,280
God, Claire, no.

852
01:08:38,115 --> 01:08:41,784
I'm begging you, please. Please, don't.

853
01:08:41,868 --> 01:08:43,869
Not again, please.

854
01:08:44,663 --> 01:08:46,747
I've been so...

855
01:08:51,503 --> 01:08:53,587
I've been so...

856
01:08:55,424 --> 01:08:57,425
Billy.

857
01:08:58,093 --> 01:08:59,760
It's all right.

858
01:09:02,597 --> 01:09:06,309
- I've been so alone.
- You'll never be alone again.

859
01:09:07,769 --> 01:09:11,480
You're with me now. You're with me

860
01:09:12,441 --> 01:09:16,360
and I have such wonderful,
wonderful things

861
01:09:17,529 --> 01:09:18,988
to show you.

862
01:09:21,742 --> 01:09:23,159
(SCREAMING)

863
01:09:37,841 --> 01:09:39,884
Damn, I'm good.

864
01:09:42,679 --> 01:09:45,931
Coop.
Coop, how long are you going to take?

865
01:09:46,099 --> 01:09:47,641
<i>'Cause I've got to get
the hell out of here.</i>

866
01:09:47,726 --> 01:09:50,394
Look, you can kiss my ass, Smith,
'cause I'm done.

867
01:09:50,479 --> 01:09:52,730
Just give me a second
to collect my tools

868
01:09:52,814 --> 01:09:54,148
<i>and we can get out of Dodge.</i>

869
01:09:54,232 --> 01:09:56,359
<i>- Two minutes tops.
- Roger that.</i>

870
01:09:56,443 --> 01:09:57,735
(BANGING)

871
01:10:02,491 --> 01:10:03,532
Weir.

872
01:10:04,076 --> 01:10:05,409
Dr Weir.

873
01:10:05,994 --> 01:10:07,286
Weir!

874
01:10:10,374 --> 01:10:11,582
Weir!

875
01:10:13,668 --> 01:10:15,753
Dr Weir,
get your ass back on board now!

876
01:10:15,837 --> 01:10:17,463
Weir!

877
01:10:18,340 --> 01:10:19,632
Shit.

878
01:10:20,342 --> 01:10:22,468
Captain Miller,
Captain Miller, do you copy? Over.

879
01:10:22,844 --> 01:10:25,262
<i>Captain Miller.
Captain Miller, do you copy? Over.</i>

880
01:10:25,347 --> 01:10:26,472
<i>Go ahead, Smitty.</i>

881
01:10:26,556 --> 01:10:29,517
I've just seen Weir
messing around on the Clark, sir.

882
01:10:31,645 --> 01:10:34,230
Stand by, Smitty,
I wanna check something out.

883
01:10:39,820 --> 01:10:42,238
<i>- Smith, get out of there.
- Come again, sir?</i>

884
01:10:42,322 --> 01:10:44,240
<i>One of the explosives is missing
from the corridor.</i>

885
01:10:44,324 --> 01:10:46,742
<i>- Weir might have put it on the Clark.
- No. No way.</i>

886
01:10:46,827 --> 01:10:49,453
No, sir, no way.
I've just put it back together, sir.

887
01:10:49,538 --> 01:10:51,872
Get off the Clark now, Smith!

888
01:11:01,007 --> 01:11:03,050
Where the fucking hell are you?

889
01:11:25,532 --> 01:11:27,199
Got you.

890
01:11:27,284 --> 01:11:29,243
Hang on, Smitty. I'm coming!

891
01:11:43,216 --> 01:11:44,258
No!

892
01:11:51,558 --> 01:11:52,808
Shit!

893
01:12:35,268 --> 01:12:36,477
Shit!

894
01:12:36,811 --> 01:12:38,187
Where the fuck am I going?

895
01:12:38,271 --> 01:12:41,273
Why does this shit got to happen to me?
Fuck.

896
01:12:43,276 --> 01:12:45,569
All right, think, Coop. Think. Think.

897
01:12:46,029 --> 01:12:49,740
All right, I got to get back to the ship.
I got to get back to the ship, all right.

898
01:12:49,824 --> 01:12:53,369
I gotta blow my air tank.
I'll blow my air tank.

899
01:12:55,163 --> 01:12:56,205
All right. Fuck.

900
01:12:56,289 --> 01:12:59,458
This better work. This shit better work.
All right.

901
01:12:59,876 --> 01:13:03,671
Here I go. All right.
One, two, three. Come on.

902
01:13:03,964 --> 01:13:07,299
Come on. Yes!

903
01:13:08,635 --> 01:13:11,220
Here I come, motherfuckers!

904
01:13:18,019 --> 01:13:19,186
D.J.

905
01:13:20,397 --> 01:13:22,523
- What was that?
- Clark's gone.

906
01:13:23,483 --> 01:13:26,360
<i>- Smitty and Cooper are dead.
- What? What the...</i>

907
01:13:26,486 --> 01:13:29,488
<i>It was Weir.
You spot him, you take him out.</i>

908
01:13:29,823 --> 01:13:31,240
Understood.

909
01:13:31,825 --> 01:13:34,827
<i>- Be careful, D.J.
- Don't worry about Weir.</i>

910
01:13:35,829 --> 01:13:37,705
I'll take care of him.

911
01:13:45,672 --> 01:13:47,590
<i>(CHOKING)</i>

912
01:13:47,674 --> 01:13:48,882
D.J.?

913
01:14:01,187 --> 01:14:04,773
<i>MILLER: D.J.? D.J., answer me!</i>

914
01:14:16,620 --> 01:14:17,995
(SLICING)

915
01:14:18,079 --> 01:14:20,289
(CHOKING)

916
01:14:52,614 --> 01:14:54,740
Okay, Dr Weir.

917
01:14:59,120 --> 01:15:00,788
You don't wanna leave your ship?

918
01:15:00,872 --> 01:15:02,373
(COCKING GUN)

919
01:15:02,457 --> 01:15:04,083
You never will.

920
01:15:34,072 --> 01:15:35,781
(METALLIC BANGING)

921
01:15:36,825 --> 01:15:38,534
(THUNDER RUMBLING)

922
01:16:03,143 --> 01:16:04,184
Starck.

923
01:16:26,666 --> 01:16:28,041
It's okay.

924
01:16:32,213 --> 01:16:34,339
I'm gonna get you out of here.

925
01:16:37,343 --> 01:16:38,552
Okay.

926
01:16:42,891 --> 01:16:44,224
Easy.

927
01:16:59,741 --> 01:17:01,784
Oh, my God.

928
01:17:03,578 --> 01:17:05,537
What happened to your eyes?

929
01:17:05,622 --> 01:17:09,458
Where we're going,
we won't need eyes to see.

930
01:17:09,542 --> 01:17:11,460
What are you talking about?

931
01:17:11,544 --> 01:17:15,464
I created the Event Horizon
to reach the stars.

932
01:17:16,257 --> 01:17:19,551
But she's gone
much, much further than that.

933
01:17:20,929 --> 01:17:23,388
She tore a hole in our universe,

934
01:17:23,473 --> 01:17:27,059
a gateway to another dimension.

935
01:17:27,727 --> 01:17:30,103
A dimension of pure chaos,

936
01:17:30,939 --> 01:17:33,398
pure evil.

937
01:17:33,900 --> 01:17:37,361
When she crossed over,
she was just a ship.

938
01:17:37,445 --> 01:17:39,571
But when she came back,

939
01:17:40,573 --> 01:17:43,075
she was alive.

940
01:17:44,285 --> 01:17:46,119
Look at her, Miller.

941
01:17:47,413 --> 01:17:50,457
Isn't she beautiful?

942
01:17:50,583 --> 01:17:54,920
Your beautiful ship
killed its crew, Doctor.

943
01:17:55,797 --> 01:17:57,256
Well,

944
01:17:58,800 --> 01:18:01,134
now she has another crew.

945
01:18:02,971 --> 01:18:05,305
Now she has us.

946
01:18:12,272 --> 01:18:15,023
What do you think you're doing, Doctor?

947
01:18:15,108 --> 01:18:18,277
You wanted to know where the ship
has been,

948
01:18:18,778 --> 01:18:20,988
so now you'll find out.

949
01:18:34,794 --> 01:18:39,214
- If you miss me, you blow out the hull.
- What makes you think I'll miss?

950
01:18:40,884 --> 01:18:42,509
That's right, I'm back!

951
01:18:42,635 --> 01:18:45,387
I'm... Shit, I can't stop. Hey!

952
01:18:45,471 --> 01:18:47,472
Hey! Fuck.

953
01:18:49,350 --> 01:18:52,352
What? Who the fuck? Oh, shit!

954
01:18:54,564 --> 01:18:55,981
(SCREAMING)

955
01:19:25,637 --> 01:19:27,012
(SCREAMING)

956
01:20:27,365 --> 01:20:30,409
Miller! Miller!

957
01:20:33,329 --> 01:20:35,414
<i>(ALARM BUZZING)</i>

958
01:20:43,131 --> 01:20:44,923
Give me your hand!

959
01:20:53,933 --> 01:20:56,268
- Come on!
- I can't!

960
01:20:58,104 --> 01:20:59,938
I'm not leaving you.

961
01:21:17,749 --> 01:21:19,291
<i>(ALARM BUZZING)</i>

962
01:21:19,959 --> 01:21:21,626
Forward airlock.

963
01:21:27,091 --> 01:21:28,592
It can't be Weir.

964
01:21:29,594 --> 01:21:31,803
I'm not taking any chances.

965
01:21:32,472 --> 01:21:34,306
You watch your back.

966
01:21:45,151 --> 01:21:47,277
- Yo! Don't hit me.
- Cooper!

967
01:21:47,361 --> 01:21:50,155
Take it off! Take it off!

968
01:21:51,324 --> 01:21:53,241
- I can't breathe. I can't breathe.
- You're okay.

969
01:21:53,326 --> 01:21:56,995
- You're okay. It's over.
- No, it's not.

970
01:21:57,622 --> 01:22:00,373
Weir's activated the gravity drive.
We got to shut it down.

971
01:22:00,458 --> 01:22:02,459
- How? The bridge is gone.
- What about Engineering?

972
01:22:02,543 --> 01:22:03,794
You think you can shut it down
from there?

973
01:22:03,878 --> 01:22:06,379
I don't know the process.
Dr Weir was the expert.

974
01:22:06,464 --> 01:22:08,423
I don't want to go
where the other crew went, okay?

975
01:22:08,508 --> 01:22:09,800
- I'd rather be dead.
- Okay.

976
01:22:09,884 --> 01:22:12,260
- Okay, we blow the fucker up.
- We blow it up?

977
01:22:12,345 --> 01:22:15,514
Do like Weir said. Blow the corridor,

978
01:22:16,307 --> 01:22:20,268
separate us from the rest of ship.
Use the foredecks as a lifeboat.

979
01:22:20,770 --> 01:22:22,938
With any luck, TDRS will pick up
our emergency beacon.

980
01:22:23,022 --> 01:22:25,315
We prep the gravity couches,
put ourselves in stasis,

981
01:22:25,399 --> 01:22:27,150
and stand by
for a search and rescue team.

982
01:22:27,235 --> 01:22:29,611
Meantime, I'm gonna go
manually arm these explosives.

983
01:22:29,695 --> 01:22:31,905
- Skipper, will this shit work?
- It worked for Dr Weir.

984
01:22:31,989 --> 01:22:34,282
You go activate the emergency beacon.
You get back here double-quick.

985
01:22:34,367 --> 01:22:36,159
- Yes, sir.
- I'll come with you.

986
01:22:36,244 --> 01:22:38,328
You stay here.
You prep the gravity couches.

987
01:22:38,412 --> 01:22:39,496
Miller.

988
01:22:41,707 --> 01:22:44,042
Close this door behind me, Lieutenant.

989
01:22:53,845 --> 01:22:57,889
<i>WOMAN OVER PA: Gateway opening
in T minus five minutes.</i>

990
01:23:04,272 --> 01:23:06,690
<i>Emergency beacon activated.</i>

991
01:23:08,568 --> 01:23:10,402
Yes.

992
01:23:10,570 --> 01:23:11,903
Blood?

993
01:23:19,245 --> 01:23:21,079
Oh, fuck me.

994
01:23:21,747 --> 01:23:23,248
Fuck me.

995
01:23:24,208 --> 01:23:25,458
Starck!

996
01:23:35,761 --> 01:23:37,429
(STARCK SCREAMING)

997
01:23:48,816 --> 01:23:49,900
Starck!

998
01:23:54,280 --> 01:23:55,655
Run!

999
01:23:58,326 --> 01:24:02,746
<i>WOMAN OVER PA: Gateway opening
in T minus three minutes.</i>

1000
01:24:06,626 --> 01:24:09,794
<i>Detonator release is authorised.</i>

1001
01:24:10,588 --> 01:24:13,465
<i>The main corridor is now armed.</i>

1002
01:24:13,633 --> 01:24:15,759
We're armed. She's ready to blow.

1003
01:24:16,093 --> 01:24:19,095
I repeat, we are armed.
She's ready to blow.

1004
01:24:19,513 --> 01:24:21,932
Miller. Miller, can you hear me?

1005
01:24:22,016 --> 01:24:24,059
<i>We have to get out of here now.</i>

1006
01:24:24,143 --> 01:24:26,561
(BURNING)

1007
01:24:26,646 --> 01:24:28,355
You let me burn.

1008
01:24:45,998 --> 01:24:50,669
<i>WOMAN OVER PA: Gateway opening
in T minus two minutes.</i>

1009
01:25:06,978 --> 01:25:08,019
Miller.

1010
01:25:26,372 --> 01:25:27,622
(MILLER COUGHING)

1011
01:25:27,707 --> 01:25:29,666
You left me behind.

1012
01:25:32,044 --> 01:25:35,088
No, you're not Edmund Corrick.
I watched him die.

1013
01:25:39,510 --> 01:25:40,969
Weir?

1014
01:25:41,053 --> 01:25:42,971
(CHUCKLING)

1015
01:25:43,556 --> 01:25:47,475
The ship brought me back.
I told you she won't let me leave.

1016
01:25:47,560 --> 01:25:49,769
She won't let anyone leave.

1017
01:25:49,895 --> 01:25:52,689
Did you really think
you could destroy this ship?

1018
01:25:53,024 --> 01:25:56,067
She's defied space and time.

1019
01:25:56,610 --> 01:26:00,113
She's been to a place
you couldn't possibly imagine.

1020
01:26:00,239 --> 01:26:01,948
And now,

1021
01:26:02,408 --> 01:26:06,661
- it is time to go back.
- I know, to hell.

1022
01:26:06,746 --> 01:26:10,123
You know nothing. Hell is only a word.

1023
01:26:10,583 --> 01:26:14,919
The reality is much, much worse.

1024
01:26:17,590 --> 01:26:20,592
Now let me show you.

1025
01:26:21,969 --> 01:26:23,261
Help me!

1026
01:26:26,599 --> 01:26:28,433
No!

1027
01:26:30,478 --> 01:26:32,187
No.

1028
01:26:32,271 --> 01:26:34,022
- You see?
- No.

1029
01:26:34,106 --> 01:26:37,817
They're not dead. They're not dead.

1030
01:26:37,943 --> 01:26:39,319
Not yet.

1031
01:26:39,403 --> 01:26:40,779
(SCREAMING)

1032
01:26:41,113 --> 01:26:43,073
You won't

1033
01:26:43,157 --> 01:26:46,242
take my crew.

1034
01:27:06,013 --> 01:27:09,849
They're not your crew any more.
They belong to the ship.

1035
01:27:09,975 --> 01:27:11,434
<i>WOMAN OVER PA: Gateway opening.</i>

1036
01:27:26,492 --> 01:27:27,826
Take me.

1037
01:27:28,661 --> 01:27:30,537
You take me. You leave them alone.

1038
01:27:30,621 --> 01:27:34,165
No. There is no escape.

1039
01:27:36,293 --> 01:27:38,128
The gateway is open,

1040
01:27:38,212 --> 01:27:41,172
and you are all coming with me.

1041
01:27:45,845 --> 01:27:47,512
Do you see?

1042
01:27:51,809 --> 01:27:53,685
Do you see?

1043
01:27:55,813 --> 01:27:57,188
Do you see?

1044
01:27:59,859 --> 01:28:01,192
Yes,

1045
01:28:02,570 --> 01:28:04,362
I see.

1046
01:28:07,700 --> 01:28:09,409
No!

1047
01:29:30,199 --> 01:29:31,241
Miller.

1048
01:29:38,707 --> 01:29:41,626
<i>MAN ON RADIO: This is Rescue One.
We have contact.</i>

1049
01:29:43,254 --> 01:29:46,631
<i>Approaching the wreckage
of the Event Horizon.</i>

1050
01:29:46,715 --> 01:29:48,299
<i>Prepare to board.</i>

1051
01:29:57,893 --> 01:30:00,270
I'm entering the grav couch bay.

1052
01:30:00,938 --> 01:30:04,941
It doesn't appear to have been damaged
in the explosion.

1053
01:30:06,485 --> 01:30:08,653
There appear to be three survivors:

1054
01:30:09,113 --> 01:30:12,407
Cooper, Justin, Lieutenant Starck.

1055
01:30:13,158 --> 01:30:16,077
Justin seems to have suffered
massive injuries

1056
01:30:16,161 --> 01:30:17,912
but he's still alive.

1057
01:30:19,832 --> 01:30:22,333
The grav couches still have power.

1058
01:30:24,920 --> 01:30:27,171
I'm opening the first tank now.

1059
01:30:37,308 --> 01:30:39,434
It's all right. It's okay.

1060
01:30:39,518 --> 01:30:41,436
- You're safe now.
- Justin, Cooper.

1061
01:30:41,520 --> 01:30:43,855
They're fine. They're with us.

1062
01:30:45,357 --> 01:30:47,025
They're with us.

1063
01:30:49,695 --> 01:30:52,947
- Starck. Starck.
- Stay calm. It's all right.

1064
01:30:53,032 --> 01:30:54,949
- Calm down, it's me.
- Breathe deeply.

1065
01:30:55,034 --> 01:30:56,242
- Starck.
- Breathe deeply.

1066
01:30:56,327 --> 01:30:58,453
- It's the rescue team.
- I need a sedative here, now!

1067
01:30:58,537 --> 01:31:01,456
- I got you, Starck. I got you.
- It's okay.

1068
01:31:01,832 --> 01:31:03,291
I need a sedative here, now!

1069
01:31:03,375 --> 01:31:05,376
Starck, it's the rescue team. We're safe.

1070
01:31:05,461 --> 01:31:08,212
- Okay, okay, I need it now!
- We're safe.

